WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.728
[MUSIC]

00:00:09.801 --> 00:00:12.767
Recruiters work for clients.

00:00:12.767 --> 00:00:15.405
We work exclusively for clients, but

00:00:15.405 --> 00:00:19.617
we have this kind of odd three
party symbiotic relationship.

00:00:19.617 --> 00:00:24.702
It's completely unique in
professional service representation,

00:00:24.702 --> 00:00:28.127
where we get paid by one
side of a transaction.

00:00:28.127 --> 00:00:31.271
But have to be perceived as fair,
balanced, and

00:00:31.271 --> 00:00:33.430
delivering value to both sides.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:34.500
Lawyers don't get that.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.481
I mean, you got two companies fighting
with each, company A's got a lawyer,

00:00:37.481 --> 00:00:38.534
company B's got a lawyer.

00:00:38.534 --> 00:00:42.697
The lawyers fight with each other, but
they know who they're representing.

00:00:42.697 --> 00:00:43.854
They're not trying to be fair.

00:00:43.854 --> 00:00:44.569
They're advocates.

00:00:44.569 --> 00:00:46.430
They're advocates for their side.

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:50.861
Marketing people, PR people, accountants,
they're working for their clients,

00:00:50.861 --> 00:00:52.655
and every client has one of those.

00:00:52.655 --> 00:00:56.194
But the search profession is pretty odd.

00:00:56.194 --> 00:01:01.105
So the people that do it have to tread
this line between loyalty to a client and

00:01:01.105 --> 00:01:03.960
respect and loyalty among the candidates.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:08.870
because unless they can put
a transaction together with two

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:12.295
willing parties who were both smart.

00:01:12.295 --> 00:01:13.031
And ideally,

00:01:13.031 --> 00:01:16.527
not going to do something that's not
in both of their best interests,

00:01:16.527 --> 00:01:20.534
no transaction happens, no search gets
completed, and nothing gets done.

00:01:20.534 --> 00:01:23.669
And that person doesn't stay
in the business very long.

00:01:23.669 --> 00:01:29.030
So you can make that work for you,
as long as you understand that

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:34.407
it's not the recruiter's job to
find you your next position.

00:01:34.407 --> 00:01:40.150
It's a recruiter's job to solve
the problem that their client has.

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:45.152
And if you can be the solution to that
problem, then they're going to be real

00:01:45.152 --> 00:01:50.169
happy to bring you forward, and
have you be a candidate for that project.

00:01:50.169 --> 00:01:55.161
If you're not the solution to that
problem, two things can happen.

00:01:55.161 --> 00:01:58.029
If you recognize it early on and
raise your hand and say,

00:01:58.029 --> 00:01:59.380
this isn't right for me.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:01.900
What I'm really interested in is x.

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:05.051
Let me try to help you with a suggestion
of somebody better than me for

00:02:05.051 --> 00:02:05.778
this project.

00:02:05.778 --> 00:02:07.504
And would you remember me for something or

00:02:07.504 --> 00:02:10.521
introduce me to one of your colleagues
who's working on what l want to do?

00:02:10.521 --> 00:02:13.232
That's a good interaction,
that will serve you well.

00:02:13.232 --> 00:02:16.452
What won't serve you well is either
trying to convince a recruiter,

00:02:16.452 --> 00:02:20.116
any recruiter really at any level,
that you're something that you're not,

00:02:20.116 --> 00:02:22.023
because they'll figure it out eventually.

00:02:22.023 --> 00:02:25.508
Or that you are interested in something
that in the end you're really not

00:02:25.508 --> 00:02:26.318
interested in.

00:02:26.318 --> 00:02:28.839
Because you're wasting
the recruiter's time,

00:02:28.839 --> 00:02:31.608
which is the only asset they
have to make a living with.

00:02:31.608 --> 00:02:34.637
And they don't appreciate it
when their time's wasted.

00:02:34.637 --> 00:02:40.058
They know every set of interactions is not
going to lead to a successful outcome.

00:02:40.058 --> 00:02:44.172
And you may go all the way to the end, and
you may decide or the client may decide,

00:02:44.172 --> 00:02:46.277
this isn't right, it didn't happen.

00:02:46.277 --> 00:02:49.734
But as long as that was done with good
faith, nobody has any hard feelings.

00:02:49.734 --> 00:02:53.340
But if it was done with bad faith, and
bad faith can be on the client side too.

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.100
I've experienced this as well.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:59.556
In which case I apologize
to the candidates and

00:02:59.556 --> 00:03:02.305
try to make it right if I can.

00:03:02.305 --> 00:03:07.167
But nobody worries about
that as long as everybody is

00:03:07.167 --> 00:03:11.263
proceeding along the path with good faith.

00:03:11.263 --> 00:03:14.884
Recruiters when they do searches
use a variety of techniques.

00:03:14.884 --> 00:03:18.603
One, they all have databases of
candidates that they maintain.

00:03:18.603 --> 00:03:22.881
Not so much from resumes sent to them but
by profiles they've created over

00:03:22.881 --> 00:03:26.883
the years of candidates they've
interviewed, maybe presented and

00:03:26.883 --> 00:03:30.540
wanted to present again,
because they've got qualified people.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:32.964
So they look at that,
that's all categorized.

00:03:32.964 --> 00:03:37.083
They look at their own prior
search work that's similar.

00:03:37.083 --> 00:03:39.652
Where did we do a search
like this the last time?

00:03:39.652 --> 00:03:42.860
And who were the candidates
who came in second, third,

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:46.490
fourth on that search,
that maybe would be a good fit for this.

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:51.636
They do industry sourcing, which is what
you talked about, where they call around,

00:03:51.636 --> 00:03:55.577
typically at a level or two above,
what they're trying to find.

00:03:55.577 --> 00:04:01.698
If I'm doing a search for
a marketing VP, I'm calling CEOs and

00:04:01.698 --> 00:04:08.876
saying, who are the best marketing
people you know in your competitors.

00:04:08.876 --> 00:04:10.308
Maybe not one of your people,

00:04:10.308 --> 00:04:13.699
sometimes they'll even suggest one
of their people that is blocked.

00:04:13.699 --> 00:04:17.494
Surprisingly it happens.

00:04:17.494 --> 00:04:21.806
Who's worked for you over the years
that you think is really good?

00:04:21.806 --> 00:04:26.472
And here, this is where if you've
worked for really strong mentors and

00:04:26.472 --> 00:04:29.283
strong performers, they know about you.

00:04:29.283 --> 00:04:32.919
And if you stay in touch with them, even
if they've moved on from where you are,

00:04:32.919 --> 00:04:35.376
they'll think about you,
they'll recommend you.

00:04:35.376 --> 00:04:37.880
because they're the people with
the reputations who are going to get

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:39.919
the calls,
the weak ones don't get those calls.

00:04:39.919 --> 00:04:43.874
The strong ones get those calls and are in
a position to help influence the hiring of

00:04:43.874 --> 00:04:47.539
people that, either they know about,
or more likely have worked for them or

00:04:47.539 --> 00:04:49.057
on their teams over the years.

00:04:49.057 --> 00:04:53.032
And really good executives I
find tend to take very good care

00:04:53.032 --> 00:04:56.622
of people who've performed for
them over the years.

00:04:56.622 --> 00:04:58.754
I mean, they'll recommend them for jobs.

00:04:58.754 --> 00:05:01.489
They'll proactively recommend them for
jobs.

00:05:01.489 --> 00:05:04.186
And in fact if you've got
a good relationship with them,

00:05:04.186 --> 00:05:05.601
you can go ask them to do that.

00:05:05.601 --> 00:05:08.577
Go to somebody who was a boss
of yours in another company,

00:05:08.577 --> 00:05:12.180
if that was a good relationship,
he or she has a good feeling for you.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:17.270
Call him and say, look, I'm ready to
make a move, who should I be talking to?

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:22.840
Who in the search firm world do you know,
that you'd be willing to introduce me to?

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:27.268
And if somebody reaches out to you,
keep me in mind.

00:05:27.268 --> 00:05:29.412
Be specific, be special.

00:05:29.412 --> 00:05:34.169
Try to be very clear when you're dealing
with recruiters of any kind, what it is

00:05:34.169 --> 00:05:38.725
that you want, what you're willing to do,
what you're not willing to do.

00:05:38.725 --> 00:05:43.846
And ask the kind of questions to try to
decide if this opportunity that they've

00:05:43.846 --> 00:05:48.671
put in front of you or called you about
is the right potential one for you.

00:05:48.671 --> 00:05:49.771
If it is, say so.

00:05:49.771 --> 00:05:53.573
Don't be cute,
don't play hard to get, be honest.

00:05:53.573 --> 00:05:56.543
And there'll be plenty of time for
negotiation at the end.

00:05:56.543 --> 00:05:59.884
If it isn't, pull out fast and
don't waste their time.

00:05:59.884 --> 00:06:05.900
Don't lie or embellish your resume.

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:07.260
My colleagues and

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:12.251
I spent years honing skills to
smell bullshit coming a mile away.

00:06:12.251 --> 00:06:12.835
I mean,

00:06:12.835 --> 00:06:18.772
a good recruiter will not let you get away
with claiming something that isn't true.

00:06:18.772 --> 00:06:24.175
That's fluffy, credit for something that
really maybe you had a small part of and

00:06:24.175 --> 00:06:27.085
you're trying to take too much credit for.

00:06:27.085 --> 00:06:28.237
You don't need to do that.

00:06:28.237 --> 00:06:32.689
You've got good records,
just be honest and and

00:06:32.689 --> 00:06:38.338
try to position yourself
someplace where your skill-set and

00:06:38.338 --> 00:06:42.586
your interests fit with
the client's needs.

00:06:42.586 --> 00:06:45.456
I'm covering some of this other stuff.

00:06:45.456 --> 00:06:48.354
The second one though,
I want to come back to.

00:06:48.354 --> 00:06:52.908
Because as you think about what it is that
you want to do, there is a role there for

00:06:52.908 --> 00:06:56.646
executive recruiters to play over
a long period in your career.

00:06:56.646 --> 00:07:03.074
And the key, I think for all of you is
to figure out literally that handful.

00:07:03.074 --> 00:07:07.063
And it's probably no more than
that in any particular geography.

00:07:07.063 --> 00:07:10.907
If you go more broadly global,
it could be a little more.

00:07:10.907 --> 00:07:12.948
It's still not a big number.

00:07:12.948 --> 00:07:18.470
And I doubt for most of you,
the critical mass of recruiters

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:23.660
among the major search firms
who would be in a position to

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:28.750
be really good career long allies and
sources for you.

00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:35.030
I would be very surprised if that was
more than 10 or 12 people in total.

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:38.693
That's a number of people that
you can over time get to know.

00:07:38.693 --> 00:07:41.379
It's not hundreds, that's hard.

00:07:41.379 --> 00:07:43.262
But 10, 6, 8,10, 12, you can do that.

00:07:43.262 --> 00:07:46.087
Particularly if you
identify who they are and

00:07:46.087 --> 00:07:49.354
have a strategy to get to
know them in a positive way,

00:07:49.354 --> 00:07:54.655
through introductions of people, other
search people, others who will help you.

00:07:54.655 --> 00:07:57.036
But get to know them in a positive way,
stay connected.

00:07:57.036 --> 00:08:00.548
Don't be a pain in the neck,
but stay connected, and

00:08:00.548 --> 00:08:03.839
build a very long-term
relationship with them.

00:08:03.839 --> 00:08:08.594
Particularly the ones that are working
in your chosen field, a level or

00:08:08.594 --> 00:08:10.508
two above where you are now.

00:08:10.508 --> 00:08:13.860
Because over time they'll move up too,
and as you move up,

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:17.346
you'll be heading into their
space from below as it will, and

00:08:17.346 --> 00:08:20.115
those are the people you
want to be dealing with.

00:08:20.115 --> 00:08:23.686
Not the ones that are working at or below
your level now, which by the time you get

00:08:23.686 --> 00:08:27.282
your next promotion, you've outgrown
them and they can't help you anymore.

00:08:27.282 --> 00:08:30.142
So you want to be aiming high.

00:08:30.142 --> 00:08:34.821
It starts by figuring out what it is that
you want to do, and being as clear and

00:08:34.821 --> 00:08:36.841
specific about that as you can.

00:08:36.841 --> 00:08:39.909
And then you get to people
in the recruiting firms, and

00:08:39.909 --> 00:08:43.389
there's five large ones,
large global ones, mine was one.

00:08:43.389 --> 00:08:47.631
And then there's a dozen or so mid-tiered
ones, and then lots of small ones,

00:08:47.631 --> 00:08:50.926
many of whom are founded and
staffed by alums of the big ones.

00:08:50.926 --> 00:08:54.495
So the quality is just as good,
they may be small.

00:08:54.495 --> 00:08:58.730
You start asking people around,
who does this kind of work?

00:08:58.730 --> 00:09:03.521
And you ask enough questions,
the same names will come up.

00:09:03.521 --> 00:09:08.610
The second way to do it is,
reverse engineer searches that got done.

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:13.573
If there's a job out there that
you would like to have had or

00:09:13.573 --> 00:09:18.449
would like to have the next time,
and it just got filled.

00:09:18.449 --> 00:09:20.143
You saw the announcement in the paper,

00:09:20.143 --> 00:09:22.564
chances are there was a recruiter
that did that search.

00:09:22.564 --> 00:09:26.225
Call up the company, call up
the individual in the job and say, look,

00:09:26.225 --> 00:09:28.974
I see you got this promotion,
or send them an email.

00:09:28.974 --> 00:09:30.171
You can reach him on LinkedIn, by the way.

00:09:30.171 --> 00:09:31.714
We'll come back to that.

00:09:31.714 --> 00:09:35.776
And say I'm curious as to was there
a recruiter that handled this search and

00:09:35.776 --> 00:09:36.816
if so, who was it?

00:09:36.816 --> 00:09:38.815
And were they good?

00:09:38.815 --> 00:09:41.064
because if they were good for
them, they'll be good for you.

00:09:41.064 --> 00:09:44.366
The one thing about the recruiting
profession is, once you start

00:09:44.366 --> 00:09:48.643
building the reputation for doing certain
kinds of searches, the industry knows.

00:09:48.643 --> 00:09:52.871
And they'll come back to the same people
time and time again, for those projects.

00:09:52.871 --> 00:09:56.430
That's how they build specializations.

00:09:56.430 --> 00:10:00.991
And the same thing happens
within the search firms.

00:10:00.991 --> 00:10:05.757
I got a lot of calls because I was
high profile from companies wanting

00:10:05.757 --> 00:10:06.937
searches done.

00:10:06.937 --> 00:10:09.085
Almost never would I do them.

00:10:09.085 --> 00:10:12.413
So the question would be,
who would we recommend that they talk to?

00:10:12.413 --> 00:10:16.618
And it's going to be the person in our
firm that has the best track record doing

00:10:16.618 --> 00:10:20.046
those kinds of searches, and
everybody knows who they are.

00:10:20.046 --> 00:10:24.731
And so, at that point, your goal is
to figure out who those people are.

00:10:24.731 --> 00:10:30.554
And try to get an opportunity
to have them know who you are.

00:10:30.554 --> 00:10:34.175
And if they're willing, just spend
a little bit of time, not a lot of time.

00:10:34.175 --> 00:10:39.005
But 15 to 20 minutes meeting you and
just saying, yeah,

00:10:39.005 --> 00:10:43.362
maybe I'm not a candidate for
anything right now, but

00:10:43.362 --> 00:10:47.170
I'm a candidate for
what you do as a recruiter.

00:10:47.170 --> 00:10:51.252
And one of these days you're going to
have an assignment that I'm going to be

00:10:51.252 --> 00:10:54.941
qualified for and interested in,
I wanted you to know who I was.

00:10:54.941 --> 00:11:00.564
And that line of discussion
works if your facts are right,

00:11:00.564 --> 00:11:03.789
and you've done your homework.

00:11:03.789 --> 00:11:10.643
It's a waste of time if it's a random
blast at the recruiting world.

00:11:10.643 --> 00:11:16.270
And just as it's pretty much a waste of
time to send resumes to recruiting firms,

00:11:16.270 --> 00:11:17.447
don't bother.

00:11:17.447 --> 00:11:19.872
It's not worth the paper you're
printing your resumes on.

00:11:19.872 --> 00:11:26.064
They get tens of thousands, most of
them unfortunately go in the scrap heap,

00:11:26.064 --> 00:11:30.542
including some of the very
same people who come into them

00:11:30.542 --> 00:11:34.855
through their sourcing
exercises later on a search.

00:11:34.855 --> 00:11:39.439
That they pay a lot of attention to,
they wouldn't have paid any attention

00:11:39.439 --> 00:11:43.974
to the resume coming in, because they're
not geared to do the scan and match.

00:11:43.974 --> 00:11:49.220
By the way, LinkedIn is an excellent way.

00:11:49.220 --> 00:11:54.467
I mean, LinkedIn didn't exist for most
of my years in the recruiting business.

00:11:54.467 --> 00:11:57.640
The search firms are using LinkedIn now,
massively.

00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:02.041
And so,
you need to make sure that your LinkedIn

00:12:02.041 --> 00:12:06.237
profile is current and
is as good as it can be.

00:12:06.237 --> 00:12:09.129
And if you want to get to somebody,
a recruiter or

00:12:09.129 --> 00:12:14.065
somebody that you don't know, and you
don't know what their email address is.

00:12:14.065 --> 00:12:15.638
If they have a LinkedIn account,

00:12:15.638 --> 00:12:19.410
you can send them a LinkedIn mail on
the LinkedIn account, and they'll get it.

00:12:19.410 --> 00:12:27.095
It's the best way to reach somebody
that you don't know how to reach.

00:12:27.095 --> 00:12:27.635
[MUSIC]

